---
name: ptt-brief
description: "Brief the user on this session's work: what happened, who acts next, and what needs the user's decision. Reads records only. No arguments."
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# Brief the user

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes (it writes none); other work needs AtE (rules file).

1. Run `<records>/bin/ptt whoami` to identify the holder bound to this
   session. If no holder is bound here, report that and stop; taking or
   resuming is the user's choice.
2. Run `<records>/bin/ptt who`, `<records>/bin/pr list`, and
   `<records>/bin/ptt inbox` for this session's holder. The inbox advances
   its usual read marker; this skill creates no records.
3. Read the records behind the items, within the holder's scope:
   - Designer: its design task (`<records>/bin/ptt show N`), its tasks'
     `status.md` files, and their open requests. Use `<records>/bin/ptt
     tasks` and the charters to identify the tasks it supervises.
   - Integrator: `<records>/handoff/integrator.md` and the open requests.
   - Task holder: its own task (`<records>/bin/ptt show N`) and requests.
   Read the status records and notes these items name, and use
   `<records>/bin/pr show N` for each relevant request, including a merged
   or closed request named by a status or inbox item. Use `pr list all`
   when needed to find those requests. Take request state from `pr list`,
   not a status record. Mark missing or unread evidence Unknown; never
   report a change you have not read.
4. Report as the rules file says: an overview line per item, then one topic
   at a time. Each line says what happened and who acts next, with short
   IDs and named subjects. Mark the items waiting on the user and the
   topic now taken up. Take up the first item waiting on the user alone,
   ending with its own decision list; the next topic follows the user's
   answer. If no item needs the user's decision, state who acts next and
   stop. Refresh `ptt who` before naming holders or who acts next.

This is a briefing only: write no status, note, review, decision, or other
record, and do not carry out the reported next steps. In Codex invoke it
as `$ptt-brief`; in Claude Code, `/ptt-brief`.
